---
// Copyright 2026 QuerySolo contributors
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NextStep from '../components/NextStep.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { pick } from '../data/status';
import LookaheadDemo from '../components/LookaheadDemo.astro';
import DataFlow from '../components/DataFlow.astro';
import AppCapture from '../components/AppCapture.astro';
import CurrentState from '../components/CurrentState.astro';

// The three doors (decisions-for-review_092426.md N2); their state comes from status.ts.
const [file, bucket, slice] = pick(['tables', 's3', 'slice']);
const doors = [
  { surface: file, title: 'A file.', text: 'Drop a CSV, Parquet, JSON or Excel file. It becomes an Iceberg table, with the type conversions written down.', href: '/docs/tables', link: 'Import a file' },
  { surface: bucket, title: 'A bucket.', text: 'Point at Parquet already in S3. Nothing is copied, and public data needs no credentials.', href: '/docs/remote', link: 'Attach a prefix' },
  { surface: slice, title: 'A warehouse you do not own.', text: 'Pull a slice onto the laptop, refreshable, and let the verdict say what fits here.' },
];
---
<Base title="QuerySolo" description="A warehouse for one. Know what fits before a query runs, know what changed after it does, and keep every table in Iceberg, on your machine or in your bucket.">
  <section class="story-hero section-frame">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>A warehouse for one / Open source / Developer preview</p>
    <h1>A warehouse <span>for one.</span></h1>
    <div class="home-intro">
      <div class="home-copy">
        <p>QuerySolo is an open-source warehouse for one machine, for SQL and dbt: it says whether a query fits before it runs, records what every run actually did, and keeps every table in Iceberg so any engine can read it.</p>
        <p class="home-beats">Know what fits. Know what changed.</p>
        <p class="home-next">Planned next: a slice of your warehouse on your laptop, a budget for your agent, and one job sent to your own cloud under a cap.</p>
        <p class="home-foundations">Built on DuckDB, Apache Iceberg, and dbt Core.</p>
      </div>
      <div><a class="button" href={url('/docs/install')}>Try QuerySolo from source ↗</a><p class="small-note">macOS + Linux · No account required</p></div>
    </div>
    <a href="#experiment" class="scroll-cue">KNOW WHAT FITS <span>↓</span></a>
  </section>
  <LookaheadDemo />
  <section class="web-section section-frame" id="changes"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / Know what changed</p><h2>After it runs,<br /><span class="accent">know what changed.</span></h2></div><p>dbt sees a changed model. Renart sees a changed file.<br />This also sees changed data.</p></div><div class="feature-columns change-columns">
    <article><span class="index">01</span><h3>A changed table counts.</h3><p>Every model knows whether it is fresh, edited, out of date or never built. A table’s new snapshot marks everything that reads it stale, because the tables and the models share one catalog. <code>querysolo run --stale</code> builds only what is not fresh.</p><figure><a href={url('/screenshots/lineage.png')}><img src={url('/screenshots/lineage.png')} width="1440" height="720" loading="lazy" alt="The Lineage screen: imported tables on the left feeding models on the right, coloured by whether each is out of date." /></a></figure><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/tables#lineage')}>Lineage and state ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="index">02</span><h3>One feed for what happened.</h3><p>The Changes feed puts the data’s snapshots, the models’ runs and the code’s commits in one list, newest first. Every run is recorded against its estimate.</p><figure><a href={url('/screenshots/changes.png')}><img src={url('/screenshots/changes.png')} width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="The Changes screen: runs, snapshots and saved versions in one list, newest first." /></a></figure><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/tables#what-happened-the-changes-feed')}>The Changes feed ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="index">03</span><h3>A question you keep becomes a model.</h3><p>It gets two checks and a version. If you have never used dbt, you just did. If you have, the model you wrote gets a verdict, its checks, a version and a state, without leaving your editor.</p><figure><a href={url('/screenshots/questions.png')}><img src={url('/screenshots/questions.png')} width="1440" height="720" loading="lazy" alt="The Questions screen in Simple mode: saved questions, each with See the answer, Refresh and History." /></a></figure><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/questions')}>Saved questions ↗</a></article>
  </div></section>
  <DataFlow />
  <section class="split-section section-frame"><div><p class="eyebrow">The files are yours</p><h2>A warehouse.<br />In a folder.</h2><p>Parquet holds the data. Iceberg describes the tables, on your disk or in your bucket, so Spark, Trino, pyiceberg, BigQuery and Databricks read them as they are. Iceberg, so the tables outlive the tool.</p><p>Nothing leaves your machine except what you ask for: <code>querysolo audit network</code> measures zero outbound attempts on the whole quickstart. The exceptions are the one extension download at <code>init</code> and the buckets you name. <a href="https://github.com/hantswilliams/querysolo/blob/main/PRIVACY.md">PRIVACY.md</a> says what is stored where.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/recovery')}>Backups, moves, and expiring old snapshots ↗</a></div><div class="folder-map" aria-label="Example QuerySolo project with a local warehouse"><div>~/my-project <span>YOUR PROJECT</span></div><ul><li><b>warehouse/</b><span>Parquet + Iceberg metadata</span></li><li><b>models/</b><span>SQL you can read and change</span></li><li><b>.querysolo/</b><span>Catalog, history, and cache</span></li><li><b>.git/</b><span>Versions of saved work</span></li><li><b>querysolo.toml</b><span>Settings you can inspect</span></li></ul><p>For a local warehouse, back up the whole folder at rest and relocate after moving it. Tables in S3 also need their bucket files backed up.</p></div></section>
  <section class="web-section section-frame" id="how-it-fits"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / How it fits</p><h2>Beside your editor.<br /><span class="accent">Under your SQL.</span></h2></div><p>Not an IDE. Not a cloud warehouse.<br />The part in between, on your machine.</p></div><div class="feature-columns"><article><span class="index">01</span><h3>Your editor writes it.</h3><p>A project is a folder of dbt models in a git repository. Write them in VS Code, Cursor or vim.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/app')}>How the app and the CLI relate ↗</a></article><article><span class="index">02</span><h3>QuerySolo runs it.</h3><p>It runs the models, keeps the tables and shows you what happened. Every button in the app shows the <code>querysolo</code> line it is, so the terminal can do everything the window can.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/cli')}>The CLI ↗</a></article><article><span class="index">03</span><h3>When it outgrows the laptop, nothing moves.</h3><p>The tables are already Iceberg, so an engine you already pay for can read them from your bucket as they are.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/remote')}>Your bucket, their engines ↗</a></article></div><div class="capture-narrow fits-capture"><AppCapture /></div></section>
  <section class="web-section section-frame" id="doors"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / Three doors</p><h2>Start with<br /><span class="accent">what you have.</span></h2></div><p>A file, a bucket, or a warehouse you do not own.<br />Two are open today.</p></div><div class="feature-columns">{doors.map(d => <article class="door" data-state={d.surface.state}><p class="eyebrow">{d.surface.state === 'built' ? <><span class="status-dot"></span>Available today</> : <><span class="status-dot planned"></span>Planned / Not available yet</>}</p><h3>{d.title}</h3><p>{d.text}</p>{d.href && <a class="text-link" href={url(d.href)}>{d.link} ↗</a>}</article>)}</div></section>
  <section class="split-section section-frame" id="for-one"><div><p class="eyebrow">06 / The boundary</p><h2>For one,<br />today.</h2></div><div><p>One machine, one writer, one folder. There are no users, no permissions, no service other tools connect to, and nothing runs while nobody is looking. That is the half of a warehouse one person needs, and it is built.</p><p>For a team will mean two laptops sharing one catalog over one bucket, with one history. That is planned, not built.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/pricing#plans')}>See the proposed plans ↗</a></div></section>
  <CurrentState />
  <NextStep />
</Base>
